>>
endobj
508 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (CTOS Basis needs to shift its primary credit databases from public cloud service \(such as AWS\) to its own Private Cloud to prevent paying high monthly charges for the public cloud. This is the difference between purchasing and owning their own physical computer servers, rather than renting them. Kumar \(2024\) says this decision is similar to house-leasing or house-buying. When you start using a public cloud, it can be easy to do, but over time, it can be very costly. This is because public cloud companies are continually increasing their rental charges and introducing hidden fees, including extra charges each time you save or move your data.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Research by Kumar \(2024\) shows that, on average, it costs just two to three years to break even on owning servers versus renting. The upfront cost of the hardware is paid for completely in 24-36 months, whereas with CTOS Basis, the monthly rent will be due indefinitely. After the servers are paid off, the expense for storing and searching new credit data is virtually negligible. This will eliminate erratic bills, reduce the risk of the public cloud skyrocketing prices, and retain more profit for the company's pocket.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (These servers also ensure that the system runs faster and data is more secure. The databases are required to be on the go since CTOS Basis is looking to link its credit checking technology directly to clients' business computer systems on a day to day basis. If these automated scans were run 24/7 on a rented public cloud, it would result in huge additional costs. The company's tech team can deploy the servers to run at peak performance without worrying about additional charges even in peak auditing seasons. In addition, sensitive financial information is easier to stored on their own local servers enabling compliance with Bank Negara Malaysia's \(BNM\) stringent security and privacy regulations. The transition ensures that data is always safe, avoids unnecessary expenses, and provides CTOS Basis with a low-cost, legal, and robust platform for expansion and growth.)
/Pg 30 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
